WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.530
- We've seen a number of
high-profile criminal cases lately,

00:00:02.530 --> 00:00:03.900
and sometimes, something goes wrong

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:05.080
and a mistrial is declared.

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:07.450
And unfortunately, people
often misunderstand

00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:08.670
what a mistrial is.

00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:10.310
Now, people know that that means

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:12.370
that the trial has to end.

00:00:12.370 --> 00:00:13.540
But people think that that means

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:15.910
that the defendant just
goes off scot-free,

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:17.030
that's not true.

00:00:17.030 --> 00:00:20.020
A mistrial would be more
accurately described as a mulligan.

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:21.700
If a mistrial is declared,

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:24.380
then the prosecutor can just
simply bring the charges again.

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:26.040
Sometimes they do, sometimes they don't.

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:28.500
This happens if there is an
error that is so prejudicial

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:30.950
that it cannot be overcome
through instructions,

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:33.690
or sometimes when the jury
is deliberating for so long

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:35.780
but they can't come to
a decision, they hang,

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:37.320
and therefore a mistrial was declared

00:00:37.320 --> 00:00:39.740
so that the prosecutor
can start the trial again

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:40.870
with a new jury pool.

00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:42.570
So, mistrial doesn't mean
the defendant goes free,

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:45.190
it just means the prosecutor
can bring the charges again.

00:00:45.190 --> 00:00:47.753
So, now you know next
time I see you in court.

